sensor for Debian/Ubuntu
========================

What installing this package changed
------------------------------------

  /usr/bin/sensord, /usr/bin/sensorctl      the binaries
  /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-sensor.rules     device permissions
  /usr/lib/modules-load.d/sensor.conf       loads the uinput module at boot
  /usr/lib/systemd/user/sensord.service     the user service (not system)
  /usr/share/applications/sensor.desktop    the launcher entry
  /usr/share/icons/hicolor/*/apps/          the app icon
  /var/lib/sensor/added-groups              record of group changes, for removal

It also created a system group 'uinput' if absent, and added the installing
user to 'input' and 'uinput'. Both changes are recorded and reversed on
removal; a group you were already in is left alone.

After first login, `sensorctl setup` downloads the speech model to
~/.local/share/sensor/. Settings live in ~/.config/sensor/config.

Why device-level access is required
-----------------------------------

Wayland deliberately prevents applications from reading global keystrokes or
synthesising input. There is no compositor-level API that works across GNOME,
KDE and X11, so sensor reads the hotkey from /dev/input and types through
/dev/uinput, below the compositor.

The practical consequence is that the daemon can see every key you press.
That is inherent to any global-hotkey tool on Linux, and the reasons to
believe it is not abused are: the systemd unit sets PrivateNetwork=yes, so the
process has no network namespace and cannot transmit anything; only events
matching the configured hotkey are acted on, and none are logged; and the
source is public.

Removing
--------

  sudo apt remove sensor    binaries, rules, unit, and group changes
  sudo apt purge sensor     the above, plus the model and per-user settings

Neither leaves a daemon running. After removal, log out once for the group
change to leave your session.
